00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.630 align:start position:0%
 
good morning  the need for international solidarity  and multilateral cooperation is  more conspicuous than ever  the norwegian nobel committee has  decided  to award the nobel peace prize for  2020 to the world  food programme for its efforts  to combat hunger  for its contribution to bettering  conditions  for peace in conflict affected areas  and for acting as a driving force  in efforts to prevent the use of hunger  as a weapon of war and conflict  the world food programme is the world's  largest humanitarian organization  addressing hunger and promoting food  security  last year the wfp  provided assistance to close to 1  million people in 88 countries  who are victims of acute food  insecurity and hunger  in 2015 eradicating hunger  was adopted as one of un's sustainable  development goals the wfp  is the un's primary instrument  for realizing this goal  in recent years the situation has  taken a negative turn in 2019  135 million people  suffered from acute hunger the highest  number  in many years most of the increase  was caused by war and armed conflict  the coronavirus pandemic has contributed  to a strong upsurge in the number of  victims of hunger in the world  in countries such as yemen the  democratic  republic of congo nigeria  south sudan and burkina faso  the combination of violent conflict and  the pandemic  has led to a dramatic rise in the number  of people living on the brink of  starvation  in the face of the pandemic the world  food programme has  demonstrated an impressive ability  to intensify its efforts  as the organization itself has stated  until the day we have a vaccine  food is the best vaccine against chaos  the world is in danger of experiencing  a hunger crisis of inconceivable  proportions if the world food programme  and other food assistance organizations  do not receive the financial support  they have requested  the link between hunger  and armed conflict is a vicious circle  war and conflict can cause  food insecurity and hunger  just as hunger and food insecurity  can cause latent conflicts to flare  up and trigger the use of violence  we will never achieve the goal of zero  hunger  unless we also put an end to  war and armed conflict  the norwegian nobel committee  wishes to emphasize that providing  assistance to increase  food security not only prevents hunger  but can also help to improve prospects  for stability  and peace the world food programme has  taken the lead  in combining humanitarian work with  peace efforts  through pioneering projects in south  america  africa and asia  the world food programme was an active  participant  in the diplomatic process that  culminated in may  2018 in the un security council's  unanimous adoption of resolution  2417 which  for the first time explicitly addresses  the link  between conflict and hunger  the security council also underscored un  member states obligation to help  ensure that food assistance reaches  those in need and condemned the use  of starvation as a method of  warfare  with this year's award the norwegian  nobel committee wishes  to turn the eyes of the world towards  the millions of people who suffer from  or face the threat of hunger  the world the world food programme plays  a key role  in multilateral cooperation on making  food security an instrument  of peace and has made a strong  contribution towards mobilizing  u.n member states to combat the use of  hunger  as a weapon of war and conflict  the organization contributes  daily to advancing the fraternity of  nations  referred to in alfred nobel's will  as the un's largest specialized  agency the world food programme  is a modern version of the peace  congresses  that the nobel peace prize is intended  to promote  the world food programme  the work of the world food programme to  the benefit of humankind  is an endeavor that all nations  of the world should be able  to endorse and support  thank you very much  you're looking at images live from oslo  the 2020  nobel peace prize in the announcement  you say that hunger is  often used as a weapon in conflicts  the 2020 nobel peace prize has been  awarded  to the un world food program  is that something of a surprise richard  yeah well i mean i think  it was not really in the top kind of  list that have been put together by  people who watch this every year  as kind of hot button things i didn't  see the world program  food program very high on the  bookkeepers kind of  uh betting uh stakes  but if you kind of pull back and think  about this examination  it makes sense so uh the nobel committee  has  a history of uh giving the award uh  two un institutions especially at points  in time where they are particularly  relevant to the situation going on at  the time  um they are making the case that  food security is very intimately tied a  weapon  of war yeah exactly right there and and  this is very much tied to  as well uh the pandemic from what we  heard right there the pandemic deepening  um food insecurity globally especially  in in countries in africa we heard about  congo  nigeria south sudan that's right so  making the point that  um conflict and food security are  intimately linked  the the corona crisis the pandemic  coming on top of that  creates a very severe risk of  of much greater worsening of those  crises  referring to some of the countries where  the world food programme has absolutely  been essential to  trying to stave our famine for instance  yemen which has been your  war-torn for years now we've all seen  the  terrible images that have come out of  yemen in recent years the world food  programme playing a critical role in  trying to keep the population  going there yeah the dependency in yemen  for example on  on the supplies coming from the u.n  world food program  um a driving force in the awarding of  uh the the uh peace prizes of course  alfred nobel's wish that  that this is tied to the peace congress  that was  just mentioned right there yeah of the  past and and there is a continuity  yeah and that was really specifically  brought up just in what we heard of  course  um and i think they also wanted to  highlight that  um they were acknowledging the world  through program not just for its mission  in itself but for the way in which it  works so it was very interesting the  very first thing that we heard  in the announcement was that the need  for global cooperation  is greater than ever at the moment so  of course that got me thinking all right  maybe we're about to hear the world  health organization you know in the  context of  pandemic and and they're using this to  say wait a minute  it's not just all about the coronavirus  there are other factors here  and i think this is also something they  quite often want to do with the nobel  peace prize is to draw attention to  issues that they feel are not getting  sufficient attention  and and this was clear when we heard  that that quote an unprecedented  crisis is looming yeah i mean that is  really shining the light on  um if in effect what was indicated and  what we heard there  specifically and knock on effect of the  pandemic collapse of supply lines and  and uh the breakdown of not only  um food supplies but the possibility to  travel in some of those regions as well  because of a lack of petrol  diesel and things like this i mean you  know the fear  of disruptions to food supplies is  something that's even hit  rich western countries this year isn't  it in the early stages of the pandemic  you'll remember here in berlin shopping  panic shopping  uh supermarkets suddenly emptied out of  all sorts of things  now of course that was quickly restored  but that was just a very very sort of  like in miniature tiny microscopic  version of the  of the problems that people face in  conflict zones  where they don't even have supermarkets  we saw other signals as well  as well as our our own need at the  grocery store there was a drop off and  in donations to to charitable  organizations as soon as this hit  uh the pandemic with people concerned  about their expenditures unemployment et  cetera  that's right and i mean this has been a  concern in many countries in the world  not just in water on countries  at the early stages of the pandemic  there was concern for instance in india  of course with an  enormous population enormous number of  people to feed  about whether there would be an impact  on food supply and security there that  did not pan out uh thankfully but i  think this is also a reminder that you  know this pandemic is still  by far not over in fact just yesterday  the number of new cases hit a new record  of 338 000 globally  so if this is continuing and especially  the global south is being more affected  as time passes the numbers we're talking  about for 2019 135  million people suffering from food  insecurity and clearly according to the  the committee the that number expected  in 2020 to be increasing that's right  it's quite interesting to think also  about one thing that the  in the announcement towards the end of  it was um where  uh uh barrett-rice anderson said um  this is something you know this mission  is something that the entire world ought  to be able to get behind  and i think that is interesting that  they did choose this organization  because  it is quite likely to be a less  controversial award it is  it's more neutral i thought this was  there's a lot of wisdom in this choice  uh  to that degree that this is an  organization no matter what your  political stripe you can get behind this  yeah i think it's an interesting choice  from that point of view because  the world is polarized the the debate  around the world oil health organization  is polarized you know the united states  just pulled out of it this year  uh donald trump accusing it of being  essentially a chinese  front of some kind um i think the nobel  committee seem in this choice to have  decided  they don't want to go into that kind of  controversy they don't want to  fuel um some of the uh  conspiratorial kind of theories around  the coronavirus which of course have  emerged in various sort of  niches of the internet and they want to  draw attention  to a major issue that is not getting  enough attention right now  you

